WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.360
MURMAN: Good afternoon.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.330
My name is Earll Murman.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.180
On behalf of the Department of
Aeronautics and Astronautics

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.158
of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

00:00:24.158 --> 00:00:25.950
and the New England
section of the American

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.050
Institute of Aeronautics
and Astronautics,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.520
I'd like to welcome you to the
25th Lester Gardner lecture.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.150
Lester Gardner was a graduate
of the class of 1898,

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:39.330
an air officer in
World War I, publisher

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:44.160
of aviation literature, a leader
of the aeronautics profession,

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.910
and an enthusiast for
aeronautical engineering.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.530
In his will, he
bequeathed a gift

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.100
to sponsor an annual lecture
on the history of aeronautics.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.070
With today's lecture
marking the 25th anniversary

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.752
year of the landing
on the moon, we

00:00:57.752 --> 00:00:59.960
are expanding the theme of
the Lester Gardner lecture

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.810
to include astronautics.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.080
If Academy Awards were to be
given for this half century's

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.660
greatest achievements,
the Apollo program

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.190
certainly would be
nominated for an Oscar.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.420
And today's speakers would be
nominated for the best producer

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.740
and the best leading performer.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:22.720
Dr. Robert C. Seamans Jr.,
a researcher, engineer,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.070
skillful administrator,
professor, dean of engineering,

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.400
and outstanding gentlemen
will be the first speaker.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.460
A graduate of Harvard
and MIT, Dr. Seamans

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.280
joined NASA as
associate administrator

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.580
in 1960, after
research and teaching

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.440
at the instrumentation lab--

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.110
now the Charles Stark
Draper Laboratories--

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.920
at MIT and five years with RCA.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.170
In his position as
associate administrator

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.340
and then deputy administrator
of NASA until 1968,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.310
he oversaw the historic
Mercury, and Gemini,

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.380
and Apollo programs.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.490
After leaving NASA, he served
as Secretary of the Air Force,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.760
President of the National
Academy of Engineering,

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.070
administrator of the energy
research and development

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.730
administration agency,
and Professor and Dean

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.100
of Engineering at MIT.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.770
His lovely wife, Jean,
his partner for 52 years

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.600
during this illustrious
career is also with us today--

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:21.347
Jean.

00:02:21.347 --> 00:02:22.808
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.940
Midway through this
lecture, Dr. Seamans

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.810
will pass the podium to Neil
Armstrong, engineer, combat

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:42.190
and test pilot, astronaut,
professor, businessmen,

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.670
and role model for a generation
of youth around the world.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.640
A graduate of Purdue
and USC, Mr. Armstrong

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.910
joined NASA as a test pilot
after serving in Korea.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:55.010
In 1962, he transferred
to the astronaut corps

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:58.780
and was the commander in 1966 of
the Gemini 8 flight, the first

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.010
to accomplish docking in space.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.370
His Gemini partner was
David Scott, a graduate

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.940
of our department here at MIT.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:07.830
Mr. Armstrong is known
throughout the world

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:11.290
for the words, "That's
one small step for man,

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.410
one giant step for
mankind," which

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.980
he spoke as the first human to
step on the moon on July 20th,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.960
1969.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.310
His hiking partner on
the Apollo 11 expedition,

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.993
Dr. Buzz Aldrin, Jr., also a
graduate of our department,

00:03:25.993 --> 00:03:26.910
is here with us today.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:27.980
Buzz, can you--

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.450
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:52.700
After leaving NASA-- sorry, Buzz
I have to cut off the applause.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.550
After leaving NASA
in 1971, Neil has

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.500
been a Professor of
Aerospace Engineering

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.470
at the University of
Cincinnati and is presently

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:05.540
chairman of AIL Systems, Inc.,
an electronics manufacturer.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.350
25 years ago, the attention of
the world was focused on a race

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.540
to put man on the moon.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.480
Such a feat seemed
unbelievable to most humans.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.410
Today, mankind debates the
wisdom of manned space program

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.690
and the priorities
for its funding.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.200
But since Neil and Buzz walked
on the moon, how many times

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.320
have we all heard someone say,
if we can put a man on the moon

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.450
why can't we--

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:31.520
whatever the problem is.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.490
The Apollo program stands as
the benchmark for what mankind

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:38.420
can accomplish and a challenge
to all those who follow.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:41.570
Today we will hear just how
difficult this challenge was

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.300
as our speakers tell us about
engineering aspects of a lunar

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.500
landing.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.275
Please join me in welcoming
Dr. Seamans to the podium.

00:04:48.275 --> 00:04:49.760
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:07.462 --> 00:05:08.920
SEAMANS: Neil and
I flipped a coin.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.470
I'm going to take
the left hand side,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:11.845
and he's going to
take the right.

00:05:11.845 --> 00:05:15.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:17.790
It's certainly a
great honor for me

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:22.130
to be involved with
the Gardner lecture

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:25.000
and to be on the
podium with Neil.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.180
And I can honestly say that
it wouldn't have happened

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:35.080
if it hadn't been for my former
boss, my first boss, my mentor

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:38.900
for 15 years here at
the Institute, a person

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.321
by the name of Doc Draper.

00:05:41.321 --> 00:05:45.740
Now Doc, on
occasion, used to use

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.820
the analogy of fire engines,
their power, their speed,

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.220
their energy, when
Air Force or Navy

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.430
brass, complete with
entourages, would visit the lab.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.950
He'd say, we're
nothing but little boys

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:02.210
on the sidewalk watching
the fire engines go by.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:03.930
And at this point,
I'm going to show you

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.525
what Doc liked to do best.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.700
Doc loved to work
with equipment.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.440
He also liked people.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:19.470
But nothing better for Doc than
to have a new design coming

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:24.300
along, to test it, to try it,
to take it up in an aeroplane,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.480
or put it through its paces.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.230
And here you see my
mentor, Doc Draper,

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.940
up on the roof of his
laboratory checking out

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.310
the guidance and
navigation system that was

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.560
to be used going to the moon.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.330
And in the distance, you
can recognize the Longfellow

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:40.830
Bridge.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:45.750
You can see the
Prudential Building

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:51.500
Now, in the late 1950s,
NASA had reminded

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.500
me very much of Doc's analogy.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:59.210
Keith Glennan, the fire
chief up front, Bob Gilruth,

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.670
there he was manning
the life support vehicle

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:03.890
for the seven astronauts.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.033
Wernher Von Braun was
driving the hook and ladder.

00:07:06.033 --> 00:07:07.700
Fortunately, he had
[? Eberhart ?] Reese

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.910
back on the rear of the hook
and ladder to keep on the road.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:13.370
He was a chief engineer.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:16.340
Other engines were driven by
people like Abe Silverstein

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.760
and Tommy Thompson and all the
various directors and program

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:20.870
managers.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:22.340
There were a lot of engines.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:24.730
And there were a lot
of fires to put out.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:28.280
Now I was one of the small boys
watching the armada approach

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:31.190
one day, and sort
of wishing I was

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:33.460
aboard when all of a
sudden, Keith Glennan, who

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:35.890
was the fire chief, he
swerved over the curb.

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.167
And he opened the door.

00:07:37.167 --> 00:07:39.500
And he invited me to ride
along with him and Dr. Dryden.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.693
I asked about what's
really going on.

00:07:44.693 --> 00:07:45.360
What's the rush?

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:46.650
Why all this energy?

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:48.960
What capabilities were needed?

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.050
Before obtaining all
the answers, Keith left.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.520
And Jim Webb became the chief.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.590
The fire intensified.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:59.250
It was no longer
merely a 10 alarm fire.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.520
New facilities and kinds
of equipment were required.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.790
We had only eight years
to meet a major deadline

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:10.900
of landing men on the moon and
returning them safely to Earth.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.718
Now we know we met the
deadline-- although sometimes,

00:08:13.718 --> 00:08:15.760
I'll have to admit, when
I see the moon coming up

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.490
over the horizon, I really
wonder if it actually happened.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:23.740
And I also find
it sometimes hard

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:27.070
to believe that it took
place 25 years ago.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.348
I mean, Neil and Buzz
haven't changed a bit.

00:08:29.348 --> 00:08:33.659
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:37.590
But the magnitude of the effort
required to achieve this goal

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:41.789
was so great that no single
person could possibly

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.520
observe the total endeavor.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.020
I say this because--

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.050
there may be differing views
of the mission, and so on--

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:52.350
but after all, there were
400,000 people involved.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:55.230
And there were many
different jobs to be done.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:59.430
Now you're about to hear two
accounts discussing the program

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.020
from an engineering standpoint.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:06.820
When I joined NASA in
the late summer of 1960,

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:10.030
the agency was
$400 million apart

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:15.000
from President Eisenhower's view
of what we should be getting.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.540
Keith Glennan met with Maury
Stands, director of OMB,

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:23.370
soon after I arrived to reclamor
60 million of the $400 million

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.870
difference.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:27.650
Stands laughed at the idea.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:31.520
In desperation, Keith asked,
what do you want in space?

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.190
And Stands replied,
a bargain basement

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:37.880
figure-- $1.09 billion.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.580
Sometime later at
a cabinet meeting,

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:44.780
George Kistiakowsky, who was
the present science advisor,

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:47.540
presented the result
of a study that

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:50.450
indicated the cost of
manned lunar landing

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:53.636
would be between
$20 and $40 billion.

00:09:53.636 --> 00:09:56.960
After the gasping
subsided around the table,

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:00.770
Eisenhower said, I wish
someone could tell me the best

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:02.840
$1 billion program for space.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:09.710
After Jim Webb became chief
I joined him in a visit

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:12.800
to the new director
of OMB, Dave Bell.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.620
We made a pitch for Saturn
boosters and the Apollo

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:18.950
manned exploration beyond the
existing manned, operating

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:21.630
on a shoestring, Mercury.

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:25.800
When we proposed design studies
for Apollo, there was no sale.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.900
And it was no sale
a few days later

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.490
when we met with
President Kennedy.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:33.610
However, Gagarin's
flight around the world

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:37.120
in one hour and 48
minutes a month later

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:43.540
raised anew the question, why
was the USA so far behind?

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:47.000
The Congress went crazy
looking for a scapegoat.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.760
Why wasn't NASA
working 24 hours a day

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.485
to get ahead of the Soviets?

00:10:51.485 --> 00:10:53.540
And let me say, at
that time, people

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.940
were working darn near
24 hours of the day

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:58.958
to get Mercury into orbit.

00:10:58.958 --> 00:11:01.000
President Kennedy had
wanted to hear the evidence

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.840
before weighing in on
new space initiatives.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:07.740
However, the evidence
had become clear.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:10.620
Whether by luck or
careful planning

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:14.580
the Soviets had scored big
in the international arena.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:18.760
And Kennedy didn't propose
hauling up the white flag.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:23.590
But which goals would permit
the US to gain greater respect

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:25.700
in future years?

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.830
Some counseled against
further manned space

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:31.370
in favor of intensified
scientific research

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:34.460
here on Earth or in space.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:37.790
Kennedy reached out beyond the
staff to Vice President Johnson

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.880
and ultimately to McNamara,
Secretary of Defense,

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:44.800
and to Jim Webb for the answers.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:48.660
Now if I can find the
right button-- here we go.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:54.900
What you have here is
that Mercury vehicle

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:58.080
up on top of the
Redstone booster.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.340
This was really a wing and
a prayer type operation.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:05.260
It was the vehicle
that was being prepared

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:07.990
for Alan Shepard at the Cape.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.110
Behind it you see
the very large gantry

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:13.120
which sort of
overpowers it, which

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:16.750
was backed off so that
some tests could be run.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:20.530
But when work was to be done on
either Redstone or the Mercury

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:23.840
vehicle, it would be
rolled up into place.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.840
Now with some foreboding,
the White House

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:30.970
allowed the Mercury
suborbital flight to proceed.

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:34.420
And on May 5th, Alan Shepard
became a world figure

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:39.950
by traveling 630 miles
downrange in 15 minutes.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:44.840
The Webb-McNamara meeting that
took place at the Pentagon

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:48.530
the following day had the
benefit of the world reaction

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:52.380
to Shepard's completely
open televised flight.

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:56.860
It was agreed by the two of
them that all missions should

00:12:56.860 --> 00:13:00.640
be conducted openly
and also that prestige

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:03.580
was a valid national goal.

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:05.330
It was felt that the
Soviets would soon be

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:07.010
able to fly with multiple crew.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:09.380
And that proved to be correct.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.820
They might soon be able
to actually fly out

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.970
around the moon, or even
go into circumlunar flight

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:16.990
around the moon.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:20.260
But it was also believed
that they would not

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:22.730
have the capability
for an extended period,

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:26.660
and with new equipment, to
actually land on the moon.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:31.270
So at this point,
let me just show

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:36.550
you what happened at just about
the time that this report was

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.130
to be handed in to the
Vice President Johnson

00:13:39.130 --> 00:13:41.800
and then to Kennedy,
there was a meeting out

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:46.600
in the back of the White House
where President Kennedy gave

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:50.250
Alan Shepard the highest
award that NASA can give.

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:53.750
And behind Alan is
his beautiful wife.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:57.990
Now thanks, in part, to the
success of Shepard's flight,

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:01.050
the President and Vice
President accepted the goal,

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:03.540
as did the Congress
following President Kennedy

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:09.420
State of the Union
address on May 25th, 1961.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:12.350
Now to achieve this
goal, policy issues

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:15.170
had to be resolved, and quickly.

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:19.010
And one of them had to do
with NASA personnel buildup.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:22.190
And a decision was made
that the necessary buildup

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:24.440
and capability
around the country

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:27.470
would not come by
increasing substantially

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:28.790
the number of people at NASA.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:32.720
Rather, major support
would be required

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:36.480
from many other departments
and agencies of government--

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.510
the Air Force, for example,
played a major role--

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:41.380
from a large number
of contractors--

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.890
and I forget the number,
but it was staggering--

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.240
and from foreign nations,
particularly those nations

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:49.615
that were located where we
wanted to put large tracking

00:14:49.615 --> 00:14:50.115
antennas.

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:57.620
Now the space task group, which
had been 1,000 strong down

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:00.290
at Langley Field where the
astronauts were being trained,

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:03.770
and where the design of the
Mercury was taking place,

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:08.870
was move to Houston, Texas and
became the Manned Spacecraft

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:13.280
Center, and now the
Johnson Flight Center.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:16.070
New operations were started
in New Orleans for assembling

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:18.340
the first stages of
the Saturn booster,

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:21.260
and in Mississippi for
testing large rocket engines

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:23.120
and boosters.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:24.890
A tremendous amount
of engineering

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:27.020
was required, obviously.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:30.170
I believe it's true that every
engineering discipline was

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:34.610
brought into play, from pile
driving in the Florida sands

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:36.770
to support what was to
become the second largest

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:42.290
building in the world to a
study of orbital mechanics

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:47.930
for transfer of astronauts from
lunar landing to, for example,

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:51.140
the orbiting craft going around
the moon-- with Mike Collins

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:54.260
aboard, as it turned out.

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:57.470
Now I'm going to
discuss for you three

00:15:57.470 --> 00:16:01.910
aspects of the engineering
decisions that had to be made--

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:04.490
first, the mode
selection for achieving

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.980
lunar landing and
return, second,

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:11.200
the method for assembly and
checkout at Cape Canaveral,

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:14.860
and third, the strategy for
evolving developmental hardware

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:18.070
into flight-ready
launch vehicles

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:20.680
as rapidly as possible,
and as safely and reliably

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:23.570
as possible.

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:25.410
Neil, on the other
hand, is going

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.370
to talk about the design
requirements for the lunar

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:29.540
landing.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:32.360
He'll get into the subject
of the rocket engines.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:35.810
He'll get into guidance
and navigation as well

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:39.050
as the subject of
training and of simulation

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:42.260
and the special aids that
were required for the crew

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:47.040
before they made their
first of a kind mission.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:53.710
So now turning to
the mission itself

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:55.970
and how it was to
be accomplished,

00:16:55.970 --> 00:17:00.610
the favored mode in those
days followed the Jules Verne

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:05.500
script of a takeoff with
all equipment aboard.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:09.630
In this version,
called direct ascent,

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:12.960
you peeled off booster
stages as you required

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:15.819
more and more acceleration.

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:17.760
So this happened as
the trip progressed,

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.369
arriving back home
aboard the capsule that

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:24.560
had been a home away from
home for the whole journey.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:28.569
A variant on this
approach involved

00:17:28.569 --> 00:17:32.470
placing the hardware and
propellants in Earth orbit

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:35.590
through a series of
two or more launches.

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:39.670
Several stages, or modules,
could be launched separately,

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:42.430
connected together
in Earth orbit

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:47.290
after rendezvous and
docking, much as the space

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:50.070
station has been planned today.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:52.920
The astronauts, once
they were in Earth orbit,

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.680
would then proceed to the moon
and back just as though they'd

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:58.710
taken off direct ascent.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:00.840
However, there was
a strategy proposed

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:04.990
by John Hubalt, the
NASA research engineer,

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:06.970
which was quite different.

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:09.820
In this mode, the astronauts
travel to the moon

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:12.280
with two separate habitats.

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.670
One spacecraft would stay
in orbit around the moon.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:17.290
The other, the
lunar lander, would

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:21.040
separate from the spacecraft
to land on the moon.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:22.690
After visiting
the lunar surface,

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:26.200
the astronauts would lift off
from the moon, rendezvous,

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:29.620
and transfer to the
return spacecraft.

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:33.070
What did this seemingly more
complex, and possibly more

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:35.792
dangerous, approach
have to offer?

00:18:35.792 --> 00:18:38.970
You know, supposing you
don't rendezvous and dock?

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:43.290
The obvious answer was to
limit the launch requirement.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.440
The return spacecraft
required a heavy heat shield

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:50.010
to protect the astronauts
during high speed reentry.

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:52.590
The requirement to land,
then accelerate the return

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:55.710
spacecraft back
into lunar orbit,

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.600
if we'd done it
that way, would have

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:00.360
doubled the size of
the launch vehicle

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.480
at takeoff from Cape Canaveral.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:07.350
More detailed design brought
out additional advantages.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:10.430
The lunar lander could be
configured for maneuverability

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:14.460
and crew visibility
in the vacuum

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:18.810
and to approach the moon, where
the visibility was clearly

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:19.530
very important.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.720
And Neil will be discussing
that with you shortly.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.000
But obviously, all was
lost if the astronauts

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:26.950
failed to rendezvous.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.410
I didn't say dock,
because the astronauts

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:33.190
were trained to transfer
the return capsule by EVA--

00:19:33.190 --> 00:19:37.510
extra vehicular activity--
if docking failed.

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:39.100
And the return
vehicle would also

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:42.400
be maneuvered for a rendezvous
if the lunar lander happened

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.860
to run out of gas or
something went wrong assuming

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:46.940
it was up in orbit.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:49.450
These aspects of the mission
were practiced extensively

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:52.360
on Gemini missions
that preceded Apollo

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:53.935
and in simulated Apollo flights.

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:04.905
At one flight, Gemini 6
rendezvoused with Gemini 7.

00:20:04.905 --> 00:20:06.030
But we flipped the numbers.

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:09.370
It was much more fun to talk
about the 76 mission rather

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:12.400
than the 67 mission.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:19.330
Here you see Gemini 7 with Frank
Borman and Jim Lovell aboard.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:20.980
And they took off from a pad.

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:24.890
They were going to fly
for 14 days anyway.

00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:27.910
And the idea was that
in about seven days,

00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:30.460
if we work post-haste,
night and day,

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:35.500
we could get Gemini 6 and
its titan booster on the pad.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:36.490
And it could take off.

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:42.900
And we could demonstrate
rendezvous but not docking.

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:44.600
Well, obviously
this did take place.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:46.350
There was a little
trouble at the takeoff.

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:48.420
We had to delay
a couple of days.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:52.050
But they not only were able
to come reasonably close--

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:54.450
they differ as to
how close they came,

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:58.950
but I believe the measurement
was made in centimeters.

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:02.180
Now, the extra
vehicular was actually

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:06.110
tested first on Gemini 4.

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:10.010
And here you see Ed White
outside the capsule.

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:11.850
And he found that
once he got outside,

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:15.090
he had a little hand
rocket gun, and he

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:16.590
could aim it in
different directions

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:18.910
and move back and forth.

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:21.020
And the idea here
was that if all else

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:22.490
failed, if he didn't
dock, it would

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:24.710
be possible for the
transfer to take place

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:29.300
by going out of one capsule
and coming into the other.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:31.600
Now finally, the
added risk had to be

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:34.030
viewed in the context
of total risk,

00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:37.180
weighing the pros of a
smaller launch vehicle at Cape

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:41.160
Canaveral, maneuverability
and visibility near the moon,

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:45.190
and the advantage of two
inhabitable vehicles, each

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:47.590
with its own life support,
against the greater

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:50.710
risk associated with rendezvous
of two vehicles in orbit

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:51.550
around the moon.

00:21:56.310 --> 00:22:00.810
Now there were many
differing views on this,

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:06.120
both within and without
NASA, let me say.

00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:10.650
And Brainerd Holmes, who
headed the manned program

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:13.830
at that time, and Joe Shea,
the systems architect who

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:17.340
worked with him, ran
all kinds of analyses

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:19.740
to try to get a
better feel for which

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:23.040
was the best of these
mission approaches.

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:26.350
And while all this was going
on, and before a decision

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:29.772
had been made, President Kennedy
and Vice President Johnson

00:22:29.772 --> 00:22:31.230
decided they'd like
to go and visit

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:36.160
the various centers
involved in the program.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:38.940
And the first stop
was Huntsville.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:43.470
And we ended up very early on
inside of the large assembly

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:45.060
hangar that's there.

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:49.410
And here you see the entourage
with President Kennedy

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:49.980
on the left.

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:52.860
You can see Vice President
Johnson looking a little gloomy

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.020
facing us.

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:58.670
And on his right is Jim Webb
talking to Wernher Von Braun.

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:01.650
There's a spy looking over
somebody's shoulder there.

00:23:01.650 --> 00:23:03.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:08.320
And then you have Secretary of
Defense McNamara, Gary Wiesner

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:09.160
and Howard Brown.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:11.590
Gary Wiesner at that time was
President Kennedy's science

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:12.910
advisor.

00:23:12.910 --> 00:23:15.476
And Kennedy was very
interested in some

00:23:15.476 --> 00:23:17.470
of the intellectual
aspects of things.

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:20.410
And right on the floor
here of this big building,

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:23.020
he started asking about the
pros and cons, the kind of thing

00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:24.880
we've just been discussing.

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:27.192
And it was a roped off area.

00:23:27.192 --> 00:23:28.900
And a whole bunch of
press and everything

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:30.150
on the other side of the rope.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:31.990
And they could see this
earnest discussion.

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.810
And needless to say, there was
plenty of probing by the press

00:23:34.810 --> 00:23:36.950
after this had taken place.

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:39.200
But when all the dust settled
and everything was taken

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.700
into account, reason prevailed.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:43.500
And the Apollo
mission was conducted

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:44.910
by lunar orbit rendezvous.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:51.290
Now all previous
space vehicles had

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:54.140
been assembled on a launch pad.

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:56.360
And when launched,
the ground team--

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.760
usually about 250 strong--

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:00.710
were as close as
you could get them.

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:02.810
And to have them close
and have it safe,

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:06.220
they had lots of reinforced
concrete over their heads.

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:07.970
So the only way they
could get information

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:09.387
as to what was
really going on was

00:24:09.387 --> 00:24:12.620
by looking through a
submarine type telescope

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:15.440
or by reading a
whole bunch of dials.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:22.640
And in order to proceed, you
had to take the vehicle stage

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:23.795
by stage--

00:24:23.795 --> 00:24:26.145
the first stage on the pad,
then the next, and the next.

00:24:26.145 --> 00:24:27.020
Hook them altogether.

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:28.077
Test it out.

00:24:28.077 --> 00:24:29.660
And while you're
doing this, you might

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:36.140
have tremendous sandstorms,
rain, hail, lightning,

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:38.250
all kinds of things.

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:39.710
And it was just--

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:41.540
as we thought about
it and then we

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:44.930
tried to plan how we were going
to assemble the Saturn V, which

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:48.620
was many, many times
larger and more complex

00:24:48.620 --> 00:24:50.470
than anything we'd
done before, it

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:54.050
was just daunting to think
of doing it out of doors.

00:24:54.050 --> 00:25:00.550
And for that reason, we thought
we should assemble indoors.

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:05.020
But of course, then you run
up against the very obvious

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:07.630
problem of how you're
going to get it on the pad,

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:11.880
and also how are you
going to check it out?

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:17.270
And here you see the
Saturn V and the Apollo

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:18.860
on the transporter.

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:21.520
Now how did the transporter
come to look like that?

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:22.960
Incidentally, in
the distance, you

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:25.280
can see the vertical assembly
building, that very large

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:27.790
building I mentioned earlier.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:30.880
Now in the past, when we moved
the gantry back and forth,

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:31.930
it ran on wheels.

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:33.440
And so we thought, why not.

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:35.860
We just put a lot of
track down, and wheels.

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:37.900
And no matter how
many wheels we put on,

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:41.080
we found that if you stopped
the transporter overnight you

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:46.290
had flats on the wheel the next
day, the loads were so great.

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:48.720
Then we thought, well,
we've got a lot of water

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.790
down at the Cape.

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:52.290
And we could just
build some canals.

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:54.510
And we'll float it out.

00:25:54.510 --> 00:26:00.150
And there wasn't a
marine type engineer

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.400
who could figure out how to
keep the whole [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.060
stable if you tried it that way.

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:06.300
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:09.260
So things were starting
to look pretty bleak.

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:12.830
The whole concept of
an assembly building

00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:15.950
appeared to be going out the
window when the Von Braun

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:19.730
team discovered
the enormous drag

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:24.290
lines that are used for surface
mining of coal in Appalachia.

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:26.990
The loads carried by the
caterpillar tractors--

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:32.120
and you can see perhaps
three or four of them there--

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:35.090
were carrying loads that
were comparable to what

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:39.080
Apollo's Saturn would weigh,
along with its umbilical tower,

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:42.850
and along with the
platform itself.

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:47.080
And the drag lines had
automatic leveling, obviously

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:50.110
an important feature, and
particularly important

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:53.000
since to get out to the
pad three miles away,

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:59.440
this whole assembly had to
go up a three degree slope.

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:04.800
However, the use
of a transporter

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:09.370
introduced other problems.

00:27:09.370 --> 00:27:13.100
Until 1962, as I've
described, everybody

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:14.680
was in the block house.

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:16.690
And what you used there
for instrumentation

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:18.340
is what you used
practically at home.

00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:22.060
You had thermometers, and
with wires perhaps coming out

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:25.640
inside, volt meters
to read, and so on.

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:29.020
But there was no way that
you could connect directly

00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:35.230
from the sensors on the
Apollo and the Saturn directly

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:40.200
into the large control
here three miles away.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:43.470
The solution necessitated
the conversion of analog

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:45.480
to digital signals.

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:51.390
Never before had so many sensors
and converters been required.

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:54.420
The same devices were to be used
for checkout and for launching,

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.350
which was also important.

00:27:57.350 --> 00:28:00.560
At launch, on the
order of 70,000 signals

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:03.710
were automatically verified
during the final two minutes

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:05.350
before liftoff.

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:07.250
And if the signals were
not within tolerance,

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:10.040
the launch crew would
investigate and determine

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:13.640
whether to scrub, or
continue, or hold.

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:16.700
Extensive digital processing
and computing equipment

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:18.710
was installed in the
base of the transporter

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:22.540
to provide all the necessary
information for the checkout

00:28:22.540 --> 00:28:25.670
and launch crews whether
the vehicle was in assembly

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.410
or sitting on the launch pad.

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:30.050
And you can see their
individual consoles.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:32.510
And at each console
you had somebody

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:36.000
who was a specialist
in a particular area

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:39.470
that the instruments
were measuring.

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:43.100
And if anything appeared
to be out of tolerance,

00:28:43.100 --> 00:28:45.730
the light would go on to
tell a fella to get alert,

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:48.010
or the gal to get alert.

00:28:48.010 --> 00:28:51.440
And by means of the
computer, and so on,

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:55.102
you could zero in and
find what the fault was.

00:28:55.102 --> 00:28:56.810
Or if you couldn't
find it, you certainly

00:28:56.810 --> 00:28:59.180
were going to hold or scrub.

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:00.950
If you could find it,
there were occasions

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:03.050
that something would be
slightly over tolerance

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:06.470
and you could go ahead.

00:29:06.470 --> 00:29:09.650
Now the last item
I want to discuss

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:11.645
is what are called
all up testing.

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:20.310
Technologies enters into design
decisions in many obvious ways.

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:23.840
But it also affects
optimal developments, often

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:27.110
in subtle fashion.

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:31.130
Rocket propelled vehicles have
experienced many failures prior

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:32.040
to success.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:34.970
Going back to the
beginning, I understand

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:39.110
it was the 71st firing of the
V2 before the Germans achieved

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:44.440
success at Peenemunde, and thank
goodness that was the case.

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:47.140
As a consequence, the
planning for the Saturn,

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:49.900
with the Von Braun
team in charge,

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:54.670
called for four launches
of the first stage

00:29:54.670 --> 00:29:58.570
before going to the second
stage so that you'd get

00:29:58.570 --> 00:29:59.860
the first stage under control.

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:02.050
And then you'd move
on to the next.

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:04.820
Now all four were successful.

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:08.440
And by the time Saturn,
or as it was called SA3,

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:10.350
and SA4 came along.

00:30:10.350 --> 00:30:12.640
The press got wind of this.

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:14.080
We had quite a
time with the media

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:17.200
explaining why we had
to spend all this money

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:20.055
to carry tons of sand
across the Atlantic Ocean.

00:30:20.055 --> 00:30:22.013
Because that's about all
we were accomplishing.

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:30.670
Now just prior to
the launching of SA5,

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:33.862
President Kennedy, who
was staying at Palm Beach,

00:30:33.862 --> 00:30:35.320
said he'd like to
come over and see

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:39.120
how things were going on with
his friend Senator Smathers.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:42.580
And here you see him.

00:30:42.580 --> 00:30:45.760
That's a member of the Secret
Service behind Wernher Von

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:46.690
Braun.

00:30:46.690 --> 00:30:48.820
And here you see
President Kennedy.

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:55.390
And President Kennedy is
pointing up at the SA5.

00:30:55.390 --> 00:30:58.180
And a model of it was
used for explanation.

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:00.460
And that's right next to Werner.

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:02.200
And if you want to
see it in the flesh,

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:04.075
you can go over to the
Kennedy Library, where

00:31:04.075 --> 00:31:06.780
that very same model now rests.

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:09.360
But what we were explaining
to President Kennedy

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:11.280
was that the next
launching, which

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:14.520
actually occurred
about six weeks later,

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:17.040
had a live upper stage.

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:19.920
It's called the S4 stage for
very complicated reasons,

00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:21.660
but it's really
the second stage.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:25.230
It would be the first time
that we'd tried in a major way

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:29.190
to make use of
hydrogen as a fuel.

00:31:29.190 --> 00:31:32.940
Now you can see that if we
were going to proceed step

00:31:32.940 --> 00:31:35.040
by step this way,
we had a long road

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:40.620
to go before we got to a full
scale launching with astronauts

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:44.430
aboard of the Saturn
V. Now George Miller,

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:48.030
when he came aboard,
answered these questions

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:50.430
in a dramatic fashion.

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:54.300
He stated we couldn't reach
our goal step by step.

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:59.940
We must test as much hardware
as possible on each mission.

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:03.210
All up testing
became the watchword.

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:07.410
The first flight of Saturn
V included all three rocket

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:12.120
stages and the Apollo capsule
with its service module.

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:18.810
The official designation
was Apollo 4 AS501.

00:32:18.810 --> 00:32:21.060
All systems were
go at large right

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:26.040
on time November 4th, 1967--

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:27.720
nobody aboard, of course.

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:30.810
And all continued to perform
essentially perfectly

00:32:30.810 --> 00:32:31.980
during the entire flight.

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:45.920
And there you see the first
launch of the Apollo Saturn.

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:49.520
And the bottom
has almost reached

00:32:49.520 --> 00:32:51.740
the top of the umbilical tower.

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:53.940
You can see those
swing arms that

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:56.340
are back away from the vehicle.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:33:00.590
And this is at the point when,
since the three mile distance

00:33:00.590 --> 00:33:04.100
to the spectators and to
the large control, when

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:09.830
you could first hear the
sound of those rocket motors.

00:33:09.830 --> 00:33:12.020
And it was really intense.

00:33:12.020 --> 00:33:15.410
When I say hear, you didn't
just hear them with your ears.

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:17.995
You could feel it in your chest.

00:33:17.995 --> 00:33:18.870
You felt it all over.

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:21.770
The roar of those engines
and the vibrations

00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:24.520
were really fantastic.

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:27.930
But the principle of
all up systems testing

00:33:27.930 --> 00:33:31.530
was established by this
daring but essential plan made

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:35.130
possible by greatly improved
component reliability

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:38.137
and comprehensive testing.

00:33:38.137 --> 00:33:39.720
It was one of the
reasons that we had,

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:43.880
of course, the
Mississippi test facility.

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:47.030
Now as I have mentioned
earlier, Neil Armstrong

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.980
will discuss certain engineering
aspects of the lunar landing

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:53.200
from quite a different
perspective than mine.

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:57.460
Neil's view was that of a very
interested and technically

00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:58.631
minded user.

00:33:58.631 --> 00:34:02.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:09.030
He was the first to conduct
a successful docking

00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:12.871
when he piloted Gemini 8
and docked with the Agena.

00:34:12.871 --> 00:34:14.630
And that's a whole
story in itself.

00:34:14.630 --> 00:34:16.489
And that was a very
exciting experience.

00:34:16.489 --> 00:34:20.510
He ended up spinning, when
the thing went out of control,

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:22.880
at two revolutions a second.

00:34:22.880 --> 00:34:25.670
And he pulled his way out of it.

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:30.179
He then landed eagle in
the Sea of Tranquility

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:32.960
with Buzz Aldrin at his side.

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:35.630
They worked together
as a team, obviously,

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:39.020
followed by a docking with Mike
Collins aboard the Apollo 11

00:34:39.020 --> 00:34:41.090
spacecraft in lunar orbit.

00:34:41.090 --> 00:34:44.150
And then he went through
a very high speed reentry

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:47.429
to return back into
the Earth's atmosphere.

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:52.580
And for his remarkable skills,
demonstrated live before

00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:56.600
millions of people, he earned
the privilege of addressing

00:34:56.600 --> 00:35:00.760
august groups like the
one I'm showing you here--

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:03.970
a joint session of Congress.

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:07.960
And here you see Neil,
with Buzz on the right,

00:35:07.960 --> 00:35:13.180
Mike on the left, Vice President
Agnew, and Speaker McCormack.

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:19.070
And now the shoe is
on the other foot.

00:35:19.070 --> 00:35:23.360
We here are privileged
to have Neil with us

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:25.880
today to tell us some
of the engineering

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:27.480
aspects of his flight.

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:27.980
Thank you.

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:29.948
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:42.357 --> 00:35:43.232
ARMSTRONG: Thank you.

00:35:52.088 --> 00:35:53.072
Thank you.

00:35:57.530 --> 00:35:59.950
That's a very kind welcome.

00:35:59.950 --> 00:36:02.250
And I'm most appreciative.

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:08.180
I'm appreciative to be asked
to give the Gardner lecture.

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:11.060
As I look on the
back of your program

00:36:11.060 --> 00:36:14.900
at the list of
former lecturers, I'm

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:21.140
struck that there are some
of the most able and creative

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:25.310
leaders in aviation and
aerospace in our history.

00:36:25.310 --> 00:36:28.930
And I'm honored that
Bob and I would be asked

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:32.520
to add our names to that list.

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:34.320
Bob said I'm going
to talk a little bit

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:37.830
about the engineering
aspects of the lunar landing.

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:41.970
And you can, of course,
find 1,000 kinds

00:36:41.970 --> 00:36:45.670
of engineering challenges
and solutions to talk about.

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:49.140
So I thought I would
highlight just that part

00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:54.930
of the flight which was involved
in getting from lunar orbit

00:36:54.930 --> 00:36:58.890
to the surface of the moon.

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:04.560
The requirements were
relatively simple.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:09.690
Begin in lunar orbit, and
end up parked on the surface,

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:12.648
hopefully in a suitable
condition to take off again.

00:37:12.648 --> 00:37:14.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:22.680
It was desirable to be able to
land at a preselected location

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:26.580
without runways, without
control towers, beacons,

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:31.010
or any other aids
on the surface.

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:34.130
Each of the sites that
we would like to go to

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:38.480
would be selected to investigate
different types of lunar

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:44.080
topography, to photograph
and sample the highlands,

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:45.420
and the craters, and the mare.

00:37:48.794 --> 00:37:50.722
I can see if the
lights [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:56.030 --> 00:38:01.400
After selecting a landing site,
its longitude and latitude

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:05.150
could be accurately determined
from lunar maps drawn

00:38:05.150 --> 00:38:08.330
from astronomical photographs.

00:38:08.330 --> 00:38:11.930
Elevations relative to
the mean surface level

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:15.210
was considerably less certain.

00:38:15.210 --> 00:38:21.100
A landing site near the equator
certainly has advantages.

00:38:21.100 --> 00:38:25.750
Equatorial orbits would pass
over or near the landing area

00:38:25.750 --> 00:38:29.710
on every revolution and
would give the flight planner

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:32.910
a good deal of flexibility.

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:36.720
Because the landing
site must lie

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:39.620
underneath the orbital
path, or near it,

00:38:39.620 --> 00:38:43.590
a site well north or
south of the equator

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:47.260
requires an inclined orbit.

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:49.810
The moon's rotation
about its axis

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:53.480
is about a half a
degree per hour.

00:38:53.480 --> 00:38:57.700
And so the landing site is
always moving toward or away

00:38:57.700 --> 00:39:01.780
from an inclined orbit,
adding constraints

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:05.500
to the trajectory plan.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:09.070
The fact that
Earth-based radar could

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:12.490
provide accurate spacecraft
position and velocity

00:39:12.490 --> 00:39:16.780
information at lunar distance
was a substantial engineering

00:39:16.780 --> 00:39:18.730
achievement.

00:39:18.730 --> 00:39:21.160
Of course, the
rotation of the Earth

00:39:21.160 --> 00:39:26.310
meant that the radar itself was
moving at substantial speed.

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:27.990
Additionally, half
of the time, it

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:32.220
was on the side of the
Earth away from the moon

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:34.830
and could not be utilized.

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:39.270
So three giant radars were used,
more or less equally spaced

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:42.480
around the Earth so that
at least one of them

00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:46.560
was always located in a
position to give good coverage

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:49.450
of the spacecraft.

00:39:49.450 --> 00:39:53.560
Because the entire orbit around
the moon subtended only a half

00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:57.640
a degree as seen from
Earth, some amount

00:39:57.640 --> 00:40:02.110
of tracking time and
mathematical smoothing

00:40:02.110 --> 00:40:06.430
was required to establish an
accurate value for the three

00:40:06.430 --> 00:40:10.450
orthogonal components
of position

00:40:10.450 --> 00:40:14.260
and the three orthogonal
components of velocity.

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:20.770
These six vectors, with
the associated time tag,

00:40:20.770 --> 00:40:26.690
was known as the spacecraft
state or state vector.

00:40:26.690 --> 00:40:29.210
It could be accurately
determined and projected

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:32.480
ahead in coasting
flight, but was

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:34.940
unable to keep up whenever
the spacecraft was

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:37.310
changing its trajectory
due to rocket thrust.

00:40:43.210 --> 00:40:46.990
In 1966, the trajectory
planners were

00:40:46.990 --> 00:40:50.110
given an unwelcome surprise.

00:40:50.110 --> 00:40:52.780
It happened during the
flight of an American robot

00:40:52.780 --> 00:40:56.680
spacecraft named Lunar
Orbiter, the first craft

00:40:56.680 --> 00:40:59.170
to fly around the moon.

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:03.520
Its orbit, as recorded by the
giant radars here on Earth,

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:06.750
had some peculiar wiggles in it.

00:41:06.750 --> 00:41:09.780
Whenever the spacecraft flew
over one of the dark areas,

00:41:09.780 --> 00:41:14.760
or Mare, it was attracted
inward toward the moon.

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:19.800
The only known cause
for such a phenomenon

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:23.340
would be an extraordinarily
strong gravitational pull

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:27.150
due to the very heavy spots
on the moon's surface.

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:31.170
These mass concentrations,
or mascons,

00:41:31.170 --> 00:41:35.820
were mapped by analyzing the
orbit of the lunar orbiters,

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:39.600
and later by mapping
the orbit of Apollo

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:44.130
8, the first manned craft to fly
around the moon, which occurred

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:48.480
during the Christmas of
1968, and again on Apollo 10,

00:41:48.480 --> 00:41:51.710
the lunar landing
dress rehearsal.

00:41:51.710 --> 00:41:53.780
These orbit wiggles
were quite pronounced,

00:41:53.780 --> 00:41:58.880
indicating that the mascons were
quite near the lunar surface.

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:01.010
There was substantial
concern that

00:42:01.010 --> 00:42:03.590
these gravitational
uncertainties

00:42:03.590 --> 00:42:06.800
would preclude landing
at a precise location

00:42:06.800 --> 00:42:08.510
on the surface.

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:11.690
NASA's landing trajectory
specialists in Houston

00:42:11.690 --> 00:42:14.360
ran many trajectories
with varying levels

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:16.310
of mascon influence.

00:42:16.310 --> 00:42:22.700
They concluded that the mascon
effects were acceptable.

00:42:22.700 --> 00:42:25.730
Additionally, they
considered dispersions

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:30.530
in inertial guidance, alignment,
and rocket engine performance.

00:42:30.530 --> 00:42:35.050
Two possible conditions
were to be considered--

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:40.820
a successful completion of the
landing or a discontinuance

00:42:40.820 --> 00:42:44.300
of the approach during
the descent for any one

00:42:44.300 --> 00:42:47.060
of a variety of reasons.

00:42:47.060 --> 00:42:49.410
The dispersions
should not prevent

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:53.230
the successful
execution of either.

00:42:53.230 --> 00:42:56.560
In the case of the
abort back to orbit,

00:42:56.560 --> 00:42:59.950
the dispersion should
not put the lunar module

00:42:59.950 --> 00:43:03.100
in a trajectory that would
make a subsequent rendezvous

00:43:03.100 --> 00:43:07.210
with the command
module impossible.

00:43:07.210 --> 00:43:12.670
The rocket engine to be used
in the descent was critical.

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:15.050
There would be a
substantial advantage

00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:18.690
in using a minimum
weight system.

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:23.970
Weight was at a real premium,
as Bob has discussed.

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:28.770
That would dictate high chamber
pressure and high energy

00:43:28.770 --> 00:43:30.810
propellants.

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:35.580
Top candidates would be liquid
hydrogen as the fuel and liquid

00:43:35.580 --> 00:43:38.630
oxygen as the oxidizer.

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:40.880
These propellants
were cryogenic-- that

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:43.880
is, they had to be kept
at very low temperatures

00:43:43.880 --> 00:43:46.140
to maintain the liquid phase.

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:48.300
And it was a four
day trip to the moon.

00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:51.830
And there was a chance of
substantial evaporative loss

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:52.530
during the trip.

00:43:55.160 --> 00:43:58.820
An alternate possibility was
to use propellants that were

00:43:58.820 --> 00:44:01.560
liquid at normal temperatures--

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:08.580
fuel such as alcohol,
or kerosene, hydrazine,

00:44:08.580 --> 00:44:14.610
oxidizers such as hydrogen
peroxide or nitrogen tetroxide.

00:44:14.610 --> 00:44:19.040
The combination that
eventually was selected

00:44:19.040 --> 00:44:24.550
was a hydrazine UDMH blend
as the fuel and nitrogen

00:44:24.550 --> 00:44:26.720
tetroxide as the oxidizer.

00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:30.530
Now both of those are liquid
at normal atmospheric pressures

00:44:30.530 --> 00:44:32.320
and temperatures.

00:44:32.320 --> 00:44:36.020
But they're very toxic
and nasty to work with.

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:39.370
They had been in common use for
rockets for decades, however.

00:44:39.370 --> 00:44:41.830
And their nature,
however unpleasant

00:44:41.830 --> 00:44:45.350
was well known and understood.

00:44:45.350 --> 00:44:48.930
The combination had an
important advantage.

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:53.800
They were hypergolic--
that is, the fuel burned

00:44:53.800 --> 00:44:56.590
whenever it came in
contact with the oxidizer.

00:44:56.590 --> 00:44:58.270
They were self-igniting.

00:44:58.270 --> 00:45:00.340
So there was no
need for spark plugs

00:45:00.340 --> 00:45:05.110
or other special ignition
devices, or other systems.

00:45:05.110 --> 00:45:10.210
Additionally, the combination
had reasonable specific impulse

00:45:10.210 --> 00:45:14.140
or energy efficiency at
relatively low chamber

00:45:14.140 --> 00:45:15.640
pressures.

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:18.550
That permitted a
pressure-fed rocket

00:45:18.550 --> 00:45:22.330
that did not require the weight
and complexity of a turbo pump

00:45:22.330 --> 00:45:24.740
to boost the pressure.

00:45:24.740 --> 00:45:27.050
The resulting combination
was conceptually

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:30.830
simple and reliable.

00:45:30.830 --> 00:45:32.830
However, there was
one other requirement

00:45:32.830 --> 00:45:35.720
that was everything but simple.

00:45:35.720 --> 00:45:40.580
Somehow, this rocket needed
to provide a wide variation

00:45:40.580 --> 00:45:43.360
in thrust.

00:45:43.360 --> 00:45:47.230
To efficiently decelerate
from orbital velocity

00:45:47.230 --> 00:45:50.420
required a high thrust level.

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:55.310
Landing required a
relatively low thrust level.

00:45:55.310 --> 00:46:01.310
Orbital speed in lunar orbit
is approximately 5,500 feet

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:01.990
per second.

00:46:01.990 --> 00:46:07.160
Now some of you will be
surprised by my archaic use

00:46:07.160 --> 00:46:08.676
of English units.

00:46:08.676 --> 00:46:11.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:17.480
Although metric or SI
was used in a few places,

00:46:17.480 --> 00:46:21.500
in NASA in those
days English units

00:46:21.500 --> 00:46:23.810
were the predominant standard.

00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:27.170
And because this is an
engineering history lecture,

00:46:27.170 --> 00:46:30.200
I'll use the English units
that were the norm at the time.

00:46:38.140 --> 00:46:41.740
Using Newton's law--
the second law--

00:46:41.740 --> 00:46:44.680
and the definition of
rocket specific impulse,

00:46:44.680 --> 00:46:46.630
one can readily
determine the propellant

00:46:46.630 --> 00:46:51.580
required to decelerate a mass
from orbit to zero velocity.

00:46:51.580 --> 00:46:54.075
We'll leave it as an
exercise for the student.

00:46:54.075 --> 00:46:57.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:57.260 --> 00:47:02.210
For the lunar case, each pound
to be delivered to the surface

00:47:02.210 --> 00:47:04.260
requires slightly
more than a pound

00:47:04.260 --> 00:47:06.210
of our selected propellant.

00:47:06.210 --> 00:47:09.330
So the initial weight in
orbit must be more than twice

00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:11.660
the landing weight.

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:16.200
When the craft was landing
or hovering above the surface

00:47:16.200 --> 00:47:18.420
preparing to land,
the thrust would

00:47:18.420 --> 00:47:22.200
need to be equal to the landing
weight plus some modulation

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:25.890
to allow for increasing and
reducing the rate of descent

00:47:25.890 --> 00:47:27.240
while maneuvering for landing.

00:47:30.290 --> 00:47:36.200
Most rocket engines of
that era were operated

00:47:36.200 --> 00:47:39.380
at essentially fixed thrust.

00:47:39.380 --> 00:47:42.350
Throttle-able engines
were very rare.

00:47:42.350 --> 00:47:44.840
And those that had
been tried had far less

00:47:44.840 --> 00:47:48.300
modulation than
would be necessary.

00:47:48.300 --> 00:47:51.910
The engineering challenge
was substantial.

00:47:51.910 --> 00:47:54.220
Two contending
approaches were tried.

00:47:54.220 --> 00:48:01.840
One concept inserted inert
gas into the propellant line

00:48:01.840 --> 00:48:05.470
between the tanks and the
rocket so that the thrust

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:07.840
would be reduced
while the total flow

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:09.460
rates through the
combustion chamber

00:48:09.460 --> 00:48:12.420
would be constant at all times.

00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:19.860
The competing idea used a
mechanical throttling system

00:48:19.860 --> 00:48:24.440
using cavitating venturi
flow control valves.

00:48:24.440 --> 00:48:30.140
That engine, which was built
by STL, later to become TRW,

00:48:30.140 --> 00:48:32.510
was selected for
the lunar module.

00:48:32.510 --> 00:48:35.390
But actually, both approaches
worked quite well--

00:48:35.390 --> 00:48:37.540
surprisingly well.

00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:41.170
The engine had a maximum
thrust of 10,000 pounds

00:48:41.170 --> 00:48:46.540
and could be modulated between
1,000 and 6,000 pounds.

00:48:46.540 --> 00:48:49.330
The thrust needed to
hover before landing

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:51.970
was about 2,500 pounds.

00:48:51.970 --> 00:48:55.660
And the variation available,
then, 1,000 to 6,000,

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:58.520
was adequate for that task.

00:48:58.520 --> 00:49:01.040
In the thrust range
between maximum thrust

00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:05.330
and the throttling range,
the cavitating venturis

00:49:05.330 --> 00:49:07.850
would be intermittently
cavitating or not

00:49:07.850 --> 00:49:13.310
and produce an
unpredictable mixture ratio.

00:49:13.310 --> 00:49:16.280
That thrust range
was prohibited to us

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:19.970
due to the probability
of excessive erosion

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:22.220
of the nozzle.

00:49:22.220 --> 00:49:27.710
Simplicity and reliability
were selected over performance.

00:49:27.710 --> 00:49:31.300
The rocket used a simple
pressure-fed system.

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:36.300
The propellants were pushed
into the rocket by gas pressure.

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:41.520
Helium stored in a supercritical
state at about 1,600 PSI,

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:47.040
and that was used to pressurize
the tanks at about 235 PSI.

00:49:47.040 --> 00:49:50.790
Maximum chamber pressure
in the combustion chamber

00:49:50.790 --> 00:49:57.120
was a remarkably low 103
PSI at maximum thrust,

00:49:57.120 --> 00:50:01.510
and considerably less
in the throttling range.

00:50:01.510 --> 00:50:04.390
There were many engineering
accomplishments in the Apollo

00:50:04.390 --> 00:50:07.360
program, but many believe
the descent engine was

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:11.490
one of the most outstanding.

00:50:11.490 --> 00:50:15.720
For guidance,
navigation, and control,

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:20.250
the lunar module had an inertial
guidance system designed here

00:50:20.250 --> 00:50:22.860
at the Draper Lab,
which at that time

00:50:22.860 --> 00:50:26.840
was called the
Instrumentation Laboratory.

00:50:26.840 --> 00:50:29.100
The system included
a three degree

00:50:29.100 --> 00:50:35.190
of freedom inertial measurement
unit and a digital computer.

00:50:35.190 --> 00:50:37.380
The IMU was the
navigation sensor,

00:50:37.380 --> 00:50:41.490
using accelerometers and gyros
to sense attitude and velocity

00:50:41.490 --> 00:50:42.850
changes.

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:47.730
The computer, which included
the navigation logic

00:50:47.730 --> 00:50:50.580
for calculating the
guidance commands,

00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:54.790
was a 70 pound 16-bit machine.

00:50:54.790 --> 00:51:02.690
It had 36K of ROM
and woven rope cores.

00:51:02.690 --> 00:51:06.760
It had no color monitor, not
even a black and white monitor,

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:09.640
no alphanumerics.

00:51:09.640 --> 00:51:12.640
All communication to
and from the operator

00:51:12.640 --> 00:51:16.430
was through three
registers of seven digits--

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:21.020
two for identification
and five for data.

00:51:21.020 --> 00:51:26.000
By today's workstation
standards, or even

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:31.790
in comparison with a Mac or a
PC, it was a primitive device.

00:51:31.790 --> 00:51:34.550
But it was far
advanced over anything

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:36.930
we had had up to that time.

00:51:36.930 --> 00:51:39.320
And we were delighted to have
it even though it cost more

00:51:39.320 --> 00:51:40.865
than a small country.

00:51:40.865 --> 00:51:42.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:47.800 --> 00:51:51.840
The orbital altitude selected
to initiate the powered descent

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:54.660
was 50,000 feet.

00:51:54.660 --> 00:51:57.360
Descents from altitude
above 50,000 feet

00:51:57.360 --> 00:52:00.540
required increasing
amounts of propellant,

00:52:00.540 --> 00:52:04.350
and altitudes below 50,000 feet
increasingly reduced safety

00:52:04.350 --> 00:52:05.940
margins.

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:08.880
Altitude errors in
either direction

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:11.970
could prevent guidance
equations from converging

00:52:11.970 --> 00:52:16.200
and would prevent a
successful landing.

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:19.110
The moon, of course,
has no atmosphere.

00:52:19.110 --> 00:52:22.200
The normal flight instruments
of an aircraft-- the altimeter,

00:52:22.200 --> 00:52:25.440
airspeed indicator, mach
meter, rate of climb--

00:52:25.440 --> 00:52:27.480
are all atmospheric
sensing devices

00:52:27.480 --> 00:52:30.240
and will not
operate in a vacuum.

00:52:30.240 --> 00:52:33.240
But clearly, such information,
directly measured,

00:52:33.240 --> 00:52:36.990
would be of considerable
use to the crew.

00:52:36.990 --> 00:52:41.160
So a specially designed radar
attached to the lunar module

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:44.880
was used to determine
altitude and velocity.

00:52:48.830 --> 00:52:54.350
The landing radar
directed four beams

00:52:54.350 --> 00:52:59.000
to the lunar surface, a
three beam Doppler velocity

00:52:59.000 --> 00:53:03.840
sensor and one beam for the
determination of slant range.

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:06.180
With the appropriate
coordinate transformations,

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:08.670
the results could be
converted into altitude

00:53:08.670 --> 00:53:10.650
and three components
of velocity,

00:53:10.650 --> 00:53:13.200
all relative to the surface.

00:53:13.200 --> 00:53:15.210
Because the radar
beams were traversing

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:18.360
an irregular, and even
mountainous surface,

00:53:18.360 --> 00:53:21.210
the radar data was jumping
and required some smoothing

00:53:21.210 --> 00:53:26.670
within the guidance computer.

00:53:26.670 --> 00:53:34.060
And I've mentioned a lot about
two guys at the Draper Lab

00:53:34.060 --> 00:53:41.580
or the Instrumentation Lab who
were in large way responsible--

00:53:41.580 --> 00:53:44.460
Dick Batten and George Cherry.

00:53:44.460 --> 00:53:46.290
Dick's here today.

00:53:46.290 --> 00:53:51.550
And many of you here
today, I'm sure,

00:53:51.550 --> 00:53:53.560
helped Dick and George
with those projects.

00:53:53.560 --> 00:53:58.060
And we remember that work very
well, and with a great deal

00:53:58.060 --> 00:53:58.560
of respect.

00:54:01.220 --> 00:54:07.340
The descent trajectory was
divided into three parts--

00:54:07.340 --> 00:54:12.190
the braking phase, the approach
phase, and the landing phase.

00:54:12.190 --> 00:54:16.310
The braking phase was intended
to provide deceleration

00:54:16.310 --> 00:54:19.370
from orbital velocity
down to something

00:54:19.370 --> 00:54:21.932
under 1,000 feet per second.

00:54:21.932 --> 00:54:24.520
The best fuel efficiency
dictate that that

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:28.270
be conducted at maximum
thrust, as we've discussed.

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:32.230
The breaking phase covered
slightly more than 200 miles

00:54:32.230 --> 00:54:34.960
in about 8 and 1/2 minutes.

00:54:34.960 --> 00:54:39.400
The last two minutes utilized
the lower throttling region

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:42.970
of the engine to
allow for corrections

00:54:42.970 --> 00:54:48.740
of dispersions in engine
thrust and trajectory.

00:54:48.740 --> 00:54:53.190
The braking phase was designed
to place the lunar module

00:54:53.190 --> 00:54:57.330
at about 4 1/2 miles short of
the landing site at an altitude

00:54:57.330 --> 00:55:00.260
of about 7,000 feet.

00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:04.340
From this point, the
approach phase was initiated.

00:55:04.340 --> 00:55:07.550
Here the crew should be able
to pick up visual contact

00:55:07.550 --> 00:55:10.020
with the planned landing site.

00:55:10.020 --> 00:55:12.740
The guidance logic, which
could be characterized

00:55:12.740 --> 00:55:15.860
as a downrange
acceleration command, which

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:18.290
was a quadratic
function of time,

00:55:18.290 --> 00:55:20.060
was designed to
bring the craft down

00:55:20.060 --> 00:55:24.250
to 500 feet just short
of the landing point.

00:55:24.250 --> 00:55:26.320
From this point,
the autopilot could

00:55:26.320 --> 00:55:29.080
complete an automatic
touchdown or the crew

00:55:29.080 --> 00:55:32.200
could maneuver to the
preferred landing location.

00:55:32.200 --> 00:55:35.260
In actuality, the crews
were reluctant to commit

00:55:35.260 --> 00:55:37.410
to an automatic landing.

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:39.390
The autopilot had no
way of picking out

00:55:39.390 --> 00:55:41.120
the smoothest landing area.

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:43.020
And it was not protected
against landing

00:55:43.020 --> 00:55:47.220
with one landing pad
sticking in a crater.

00:55:47.220 --> 00:55:51.240
Analytical studies had indicated
7% of automatic landings

00:55:51.240 --> 00:55:52.680
would overturn on touchdown.

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:55.770
[LAUGHTER] So it's
not surprising

00:55:55.770 --> 00:55:59.645
that the Apollo landings
were flown manually.

00:56:04.010 --> 00:56:07.870
Now, I put this diagram in
because when I was teaching,

00:56:07.870 --> 00:56:11.611
these are the quality of
the slides I used to use.

00:56:11.611 --> 00:56:16.270
[LAUGHTER] Because of
the reduced gravity,

00:56:16.270 --> 00:56:19.630
a machine hovering over the
moon, as shown in the middle

00:56:19.630 --> 00:56:23.200
there, would require only
1/6 of the vertical thrust

00:56:23.200 --> 00:56:27.760
it would need hovering over
Earth, as on the left side.

00:56:27.760 --> 00:56:30.840
So while slowing to a
hover, the forward component

00:56:30.840 --> 00:56:37.560
of that thrust is consequently
only 1/6 of its value on Earth.

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:41.760
And likewise, the
deceleration is only 1/6.

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:46.440
So to obtain the same
translational acceleration

00:56:46.440 --> 00:56:51.120
or deceleration as you would
get on Earth on the left that

00:56:51.120 --> 00:56:55.080
requires attitude
changes, as shown

00:56:55.080 --> 00:56:57.150
on the far right side,
that are approximately

00:56:57.150 --> 00:57:00.900
six times larger than
would be required on Earth.

00:57:00.900 --> 00:57:03.870
Traversing large pitch or
roll angles, of course,

00:57:03.870 --> 00:57:08.880
either requires more time
or larger control powers.

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:11.550
It was expected that the
control characteristics

00:57:11.550 --> 00:57:18.200
ideal here on Earth would not at
all be acceptable on the moon.

00:57:18.200 --> 00:57:21.610
These concerns had been the
subject of much attention

00:57:21.610 --> 00:57:26.110
in the years immediately
prior to the landing attempt.

00:57:26.110 --> 00:57:30.570
A variety of studies and
simulations had been conducted.

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:34.110
Attitude changes in
roll, and pitch, in yaw

00:57:34.110 --> 00:57:37.650
were assumed to be achieved
by the use of small attitude

00:57:37.650 --> 00:57:39.680
control rockets.

00:57:39.680 --> 00:57:42.500
In the ground-based
flight simulators,

00:57:42.500 --> 00:57:46.190
pilots found that they
could control attitude well

00:57:46.190 --> 00:57:48.350
with reaction control rockets.

00:57:48.350 --> 00:57:52.910
But they encountered difficulty
in making precise landings

00:57:52.910 --> 00:57:57.110
and eliminating residual
velocities at touchdown.

00:57:57.110 --> 00:58:00.290
It was clear that something
nearer to the real flying

00:58:00.290 --> 00:58:02.850
experience was needed.

00:58:02.850 --> 00:58:05.040
Helicopters were tried.

00:58:05.040 --> 00:58:05.790
They could hover.

00:58:05.790 --> 00:58:10.440
And they could duplicate the
final lunar landing trajectory.

00:58:10.440 --> 00:58:14.460
Unfortunately, they were
incapable of investigating

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:17.550
the problems of most concern.

00:58:17.550 --> 00:58:20.490
They could neither
replicate the consequences

00:58:20.490 --> 00:58:23.130
of the lunar gravity
or the handling

00:58:23.130 --> 00:58:27.540
characteristics of reaction
control system machines.

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:29.500
Fortunately, there
were two projects

00:58:29.500 --> 00:58:33.540
which very effectively filled
the void-- the Lunar Landing

00:58:33.540 --> 00:58:37.770
Research Vehicle, LLRV, and the
Lunar Landing Research Facility

00:58:37.770 --> 00:58:38.270
LLRF.

00:58:41.450 --> 00:58:45.860
The LLRV was developed by
NASA's flight research center

00:58:45.860 --> 00:58:50.150
at Edwards California and built
by the Bell Aircraft Company.

00:58:50.150 --> 00:58:53.630
In the lunar simulation
mode, the turbo fan engine,

00:58:53.630 --> 00:58:56.090
mounted in a double
gimbal, was aligned

00:58:56.090 --> 00:58:59.150
to the local vertical
and the thrust adjusted

00:58:59.150 --> 00:59:04.200
to 5/6 of the LLRV weight.

00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:07.090
The equivalent of the
lunar module descent engine

00:59:07.090 --> 00:59:12.490
was provided by a pair of
throttle-able 500 pound thrust

00:59:12.490 --> 00:59:16.840
rockets fixed to the fuselage
outside the gimbal ring.

00:59:16.840 --> 00:59:20.470
These were monopropellant
rockets using hydrogen peroxide

00:59:20.470 --> 00:59:21.990
as a fuel.

00:59:21.990 --> 00:59:24.220
The turbo fans angle
and thrust could

00:59:24.220 --> 00:59:27.520
be modulated to compensate
for aerodynamic drag

00:59:27.520 --> 00:59:33.210
so that the craft coasted as
if it were flying in a vacuum.

00:59:33.210 --> 00:59:36.100
The LLRV had a very
lightweight structure

00:59:36.100 --> 00:59:40.450
and encountered a variety of
interactions between controls

00:59:40.450 --> 00:59:42.790
and low structural frequencies.

00:59:42.790 --> 00:59:46.120
After a number of development
changes were incorporated,

00:59:46.120 --> 00:59:50.470
this cleverly compensated
flying machine

00:59:50.470 --> 00:59:53.410
actually managed to
closely duplicate

00:59:53.410 --> 00:59:55.630
flying as if it
were over the moon,

00:59:55.630 --> 00:59:59.140
and became a very useful
tool for understanding

00:59:59.140 --> 01:00:01.910
the needs of flying in
the lunar environment.

01:00:05.910 --> 01:00:09.420
At the NASA Langley
Research Center,

01:00:09.420 --> 01:00:13.290
an alternate approach
to lunar simulation

01:00:13.290 --> 01:00:16.470
was initiated in what they
called the Lunar Landing

01:00:16.470 --> 01:00:17.460
Research Facility.

01:00:17.460 --> 01:00:22.920
I don't have a slide, I'm sorry,
but you can imagine a craft

01:00:22.920 --> 01:00:27.510
like one of those stick figures
I drew suspended by cables

01:00:27.510 --> 01:00:31.010
from a large gantry crane.

01:00:31.010 --> 01:00:34.580
The idea, again, was to
duplicate the lunar gravity

01:00:34.580 --> 01:00:37.100
and control characteristics.

01:00:37.100 --> 01:00:41.780
The method was to lower
the vehicle apparent weight

01:00:41.780 --> 01:00:45.170
to its lunar equivalent
by lifting upward

01:00:45.170 --> 01:00:50.520
on the vertical cables attached
to a traveling bridge crane.

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:54.740
And a complex
electrohydraulic system

01:00:54.740 --> 01:00:59.040
kept the crane platform
directly over the machine

01:00:59.040 --> 01:01:02.570
and with an upward
force equal to 5/6

01:01:02.570 --> 01:01:05.150
of the vehicle weight,
even during translations,

01:01:05.150 --> 01:01:09.770
and angular motions, and weight
changes during due to fuel

01:01:09.770 --> 01:01:11.150
usage.

01:01:11.150 --> 01:01:17.750
It had a wonderful combination
of structural cable stretch,

01:01:17.750 --> 01:01:22.370
pendulous frequencies requiring
innovative compensation

01:01:22.370 --> 01:01:23.010
techniques.

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:26.632
It, indeed, was a
mechanical engineer's dream.

01:01:26.632 --> 01:01:28.560
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:30.770
The vehicle was
attached to the cables

01:01:30.770 --> 01:01:34.460
through gimbal rings allowing
pitch, roll, and yaw motion

01:01:34.460 --> 01:01:37.190
provided by hydrogen
peroxide rockets.

01:01:37.190 --> 01:01:42.170
And the 1/6 vehicle weight
not lifted by the cable system

01:01:42.170 --> 01:01:44.750
was lifted by a throttle-able
hydrogen peroxide

01:01:44.750 --> 01:01:47.300
rocket fixed to the structure.

01:01:47.300 --> 01:01:52.190
After the kinks were worked out,
it worked surprisingly well.

01:01:52.190 --> 01:01:57.050
The flying volume, which was
about 180 feet high, 360 feet

01:01:57.050 --> 01:02:00.050
long, and 42 feet
wide was somewhat

01:02:00.050 --> 01:02:03.980
limiting for a pilot,
but adequate to give

01:02:03.980 --> 01:02:09.760
a substantive introduction to
lunar flight characteristics.

01:02:09.760 --> 01:02:13.690
Both the LLRV and the LLRF
were able to investigate

01:02:13.690 --> 01:02:17.290
a wide range of control
characteristics.

01:02:17.290 --> 01:02:20.140
Control system
parameters were varied

01:02:20.140 --> 01:02:23.020
and optimum combinations
recommended, primarily

01:02:23.020 --> 01:02:25.670
based on pilot opinion.

01:02:25.670 --> 01:02:31.210
Both LLRV LLRF studies indicated
that much lower control powers

01:02:31.210 --> 01:02:36.610
were acceptable than had been
expected based on VTOL aircraft

01:02:36.610 --> 01:02:38.470
and helicopter experience.

01:02:38.470 --> 01:02:43.900
It was a welcome surprise,
because low control powers

01:02:43.900 --> 01:02:47.630
result in low attitude
control fuel consumption.

01:02:47.630 --> 01:02:49.810
These and related
evaluations were

01:02:49.810 --> 01:02:53.800
most useful in establishing
the actual lunar module control

01:02:53.800 --> 01:02:55.610
configuration.

01:02:55.610 --> 01:02:59.810
The final LEM attitude control
system was rate command,

01:02:59.810 --> 01:03:01.190
attitude hold.

01:03:01.190 --> 01:03:04.190
That is the control stick
commanded angular rate

01:03:04.190 --> 01:03:07.100
in all three axes,
but when centered,

01:03:07.100 --> 01:03:10.110
attitude was maintained.

01:03:10.110 --> 01:03:13.350
Based on the studies I've
just been talking about,

01:03:13.350 --> 01:03:16.770
pitch and roll control
power had been established

01:03:16.770 --> 01:03:19.950
at eight degrees per second,
and maximum commanded

01:03:19.950 --> 01:03:27.440
rates at 20 degrees per
second for the lunar module.

01:03:27.440 --> 01:03:29.630
And that provided
handling qualities

01:03:29.630 --> 01:03:32.760
that were remarkably good.

01:03:32.760 --> 01:03:36.560
In the LLRV, here, the
rocket was directly

01:03:36.560 --> 01:03:38.570
controlled by the throttle.

01:03:38.570 --> 01:03:40.100
But the lunar module--

01:03:40.100 --> 01:03:43.220
the real lunar module--
added an automatic throttle

01:03:43.220 --> 01:03:46.610
that would modulate thrust to
maintain a constant descent

01:03:46.610 --> 01:03:47.800
rate.

01:03:47.800 --> 01:03:49.990
Workload was
substantially reduced

01:03:49.990 --> 01:03:52.930
and gave the pilot more time
to concentrate on selecting

01:03:52.930 --> 01:03:56.050
a suitable landing spot.

01:03:56.050 --> 01:04:01.670
A number of concerns
regarding lunar flying

01:04:01.670 --> 01:04:04.025
emerged that were
unrelated to the hardware.

01:04:06.740 --> 01:04:09.110
For many decades,
astronomers were

01:04:09.110 --> 01:04:12.980
mystified by the peculiar
reflective qualities

01:04:12.980 --> 01:04:14.790
of the moon.

01:04:14.790 --> 01:04:19.340
These are surprisingly large
changes in brightness and sun

01:04:19.340 --> 01:04:22.730
angle and a very bright
spot along the sun line.

01:04:22.730 --> 01:04:25.940
It was feared that
visibility, visual acuity,

01:04:25.940 --> 01:04:28.220
and depth perception
during a lunar landing

01:04:28.220 --> 01:04:30.810
could be severely degraded.

01:04:30.810 --> 01:04:33.930
Throughout the Apollo
program development,

01:04:33.930 --> 01:04:36.990
there were worries about
visibility degradations

01:04:36.990 --> 01:04:41.700
due to dust clouds caused by
the high velocity rocket exhaust

01:04:41.700 --> 01:04:44.170
impinging on the lunar surface.

01:04:44.170 --> 01:04:47.710
Additionally, rough
country, a close horizon,

01:04:47.710 --> 01:04:50.220
and a lack of a strong
sense of the vertical

01:04:50.220 --> 01:04:54.060
were possible impediments
to precision flying.

01:04:54.060 --> 01:04:58.380
The lunar module experienced
normal design evolution.

01:04:58.380 --> 01:05:01.940
The cabin volume was changed
from spherical to cylindrical.

01:05:01.940 --> 01:05:05.010
The landing legs were
reduced from five to four.

01:05:05.010 --> 01:05:08.070
The window area was
substantially reduced.

01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:09.708
And the seat was removed.

01:05:09.708 --> 01:05:11.202
[LAUGHTER]

01:05:11.202 --> 01:05:14.880
The standing pilot, or
trolley conductor cockpit,

01:05:14.880 --> 01:05:17.490
reduced weight,
moved the pilot's eye

01:05:17.490 --> 01:05:21.240
closer to the window,
improving visibility,

01:05:21.240 --> 01:05:23.100
and improved the structure.

01:05:23.100 --> 01:05:25.200
A cable restraint
system was devised

01:05:25.200 --> 01:05:28.200
to keep the pilots in the
proper position in the cabin,

01:05:28.200 --> 01:05:32.340
and to hold them secure
during landing loads.

01:05:32.340 --> 01:05:36.850
The fears about being blinded
by the sun's reflection

01:05:36.850 --> 01:05:38.810
were unfounded.

01:05:38.810 --> 01:05:42.590
The bright spot was
there, but the human eye

01:05:42.590 --> 01:05:46.070
is quite capable of
accommodating to it.

01:05:46.070 --> 01:05:49.580
And it did not
hinder operations.

01:05:49.580 --> 01:05:53.360
The initial flights landed
early in the lunar morning.

01:05:53.360 --> 01:05:55.700
That provided long
shadows, which

01:05:55.700 --> 01:05:58.560
improved depth perception.

01:05:58.560 --> 01:06:00.570
Lunar dust, on the
other hand, proved

01:06:00.570 --> 01:06:03.410
to be a substantial impediment.

01:06:03.410 --> 01:06:07.260
The dust was found
not to erupt in clouds

01:06:07.260 --> 01:06:10.020
as it would here on Earth.

01:06:10.020 --> 01:06:13.560
It travels radially outward
from the exhaust impingement

01:06:13.560 --> 01:06:18.180
area in horizontal
thin, flat sheets.

01:06:18.180 --> 01:06:20.520
It's very logical
in the lunar vacuum.

01:06:20.520 --> 01:06:26.070
But it's a most surprising
phenomenon to Earthlings.

01:06:26.070 --> 01:06:30.010
When the rocket engine
stops, the dust immediately

01:06:30.010 --> 01:06:33.010
disappears over the
horizon, leaving

01:06:33.010 --> 01:06:35.950
a perfectly clear view
with no dust cloud

01:06:35.950 --> 01:06:39.750
or other residual
effects of any sort.

01:06:39.750 --> 01:06:43.470
As might be expected,
the dust quantity

01:06:43.470 --> 01:06:47.430
varied from landing
site to landing site.

01:06:47.430 --> 01:06:53.800
On Apollo 11, Buzz and I had
a dust sheet that was opaque,

01:06:53.800 --> 01:06:57.600
and I would say disconcerting,
due to its rapid movement.

01:06:57.600 --> 01:07:00.120
But boulders protruding
above it provided

01:07:00.120 --> 01:07:05.420
benchmarks adequate for
estimating speed and altitude.

01:07:05.420 --> 01:07:12.120
On Apollos 14, 16, and 17,
the dust was quite thin,

01:07:12.120 --> 01:07:15.900
and permitted visual
reference to the surface.

01:07:15.900 --> 01:07:19.560
On Apollos 12 and 15,
however, the surface

01:07:19.560 --> 01:07:23.730
was completely obscured with
dust below 50 feet of altitude,

01:07:23.730 --> 01:07:27.117
and the landings were completed
essentially on instruments.

01:07:29.800 --> 01:07:36.610
All in all, in spite of
its ungainly appearance,

01:07:36.610 --> 01:07:39.982
the lunar module was a
superb flying machine.

01:07:39.982 --> 01:07:43.340
It was a credit to the
engineering profession

01:07:43.340 --> 01:07:50.630
and to those Grumman, TRW,
Draper Lab, and the others

01:07:50.630 --> 01:07:52.050
who created it.

01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:55.040
I'm in the debt of
all those folks.

01:07:55.040 --> 01:07:58.265
And I thank you all for the
chance to join you today.

01:07:58.265 --> 01:08:00.245
[APPLAUSE]

01:08:45.912 --> 01:08:47.370
MURMAN: I'm sure
our speakers would

01:08:47.370 --> 01:08:49.319
be happy to take
a few questions,

01:08:49.319 --> 01:08:53.770
if maybe I could invite
Bob and Neil to come back

01:08:53.770 --> 01:08:54.359
to the podium.

01:08:54.359 --> 01:08:55.870
And I'll sit down.

01:08:55.870 --> 01:08:58.170
And I would like to ask
that those of you who

01:08:58.170 --> 01:09:00.990
want to ask questions come
forward to the microphones,

01:09:00.990 --> 01:09:05.279
because this is being televised
across the campus cable TV

01:09:05.279 --> 01:09:06.018
system.

01:09:06.018 --> 01:09:07.560
And we'll have a
couple of questions.

01:09:07.560 --> 01:09:10.199
And then we'll conclude it.

01:09:18.467 --> 01:09:20.050
AUDIENCE: Mr.
Armstrong, I'm wondering

01:09:20.050 --> 01:09:25.260
if you were concerned at
all that the funding--

01:09:25.260 --> 01:09:27.670
with funding levels for the
space program in this country

01:09:27.670 --> 01:09:29.080
have left it in decline?

01:09:29.080 --> 01:09:31.540
Or do you think that things
are moving along well

01:09:31.540 --> 01:09:33.064
from your point of view?

01:09:33.064 --> 01:09:34.689
ARMSTRONG: Well,
that's a question that

01:09:34.689 --> 01:09:38.330
has to do with
history in the future.

01:09:38.330 --> 01:09:45.649
And it's really a bit outside
the scope of this lecture.

01:09:45.649 --> 01:09:49.870
But I would just say that
I think all of us that

01:09:49.870 --> 01:09:55.930
have been involved in these
programs over the past quarter

01:09:55.930 --> 01:09:59.110
century or so have been
very pleased with the things

01:09:59.110 --> 01:10:00.160
we have accomplished.

01:10:00.160 --> 01:10:03.850
We're a little disappointed
that more things aren't

01:10:03.850 --> 01:10:10.880
being initiated and less new
projects are being supported.

01:10:10.880 --> 01:10:15.940
But it's my hope that that's
a temporary situation.

01:10:15.940 --> 01:10:17.980
The world is full of
hills and valleys.

01:10:17.980 --> 01:10:19.840
And that will never change.

01:10:19.840 --> 01:10:23.320
AUDIENCE: Is there any
feeling you have about where

01:10:23.320 --> 01:10:24.880
funding should be directed?

01:10:24.880 --> 01:10:27.370
Or is there any particular
program or other programs

01:10:27.370 --> 01:10:29.140
that you think
should be priorities?

01:10:29.140 --> 01:10:30.848
And then I promise
I'll let you get away.

01:10:35.850 --> 01:10:41.880
ARMSTRONG: Well, I think
I feel comfortable arguing

01:10:41.880 --> 01:10:45.750
in favor of any one of a
number of new elements.

01:10:45.750 --> 01:10:48.578
I'm not going to use today
to pick one over the other.

01:10:51.883 --> 01:10:53.300
AUDIENCE: I wonder
if you might be

01:10:53.300 --> 01:10:55.610
able to recount some
of the last moments

01:10:55.610 --> 01:10:57.200
up to the lunar landing.

01:10:57.200 --> 01:11:00.737
Specifically, I understand
the radar picked up

01:11:00.737 --> 01:11:02.570
a number of boulders
over your landing site.

01:11:02.570 --> 01:11:04.310
And you had to hover
for quite a while,

01:11:04.310 --> 01:11:06.048
and nearly ran out
of fuel, and almost

01:11:06.048 --> 01:11:07.090
had to abort the mission.

01:11:07.090 --> 01:11:09.050
I wonder if you could
recount how close you

01:11:09.050 --> 01:11:12.521
were to aborting the mission
in some of those last moments?

01:11:15.860 --> 01:11:19.550
ARMSTRONG: I'm not sure
you could all hear that,

01:11:19.550 --> 01:11:27.470
but the question was directed
toward the final part

01:11:27.470 --> 01:11:32.790
of the Apollo 11 landing
where we did have a--

01:11:32.790 --> 01:11:36.080
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:36.080 --> 01:11:42.380
--some amount of divergence
to keep our minds occupied.

01:11:42.380 --> 01:11:46.250
Fortunately, Buzz was a
master on the computer.

01:11:46.250 --> 01:11:52.140
And he was able to keep me from
worrying about that too much.

01:11:52.140 --> 01:11:56.270
We were flying
into the computer--

01:11:56.270 --> 01:11:58.760
the autopilot was
flying us into an area.

01:11:58.760 --> 01:12:01.760
As I said, it couldn't
know where it was going.

01:12:01.760 --> 01:12:07.980
And it picked a very rough
spot, a very large crater--

01:12:07.980 --> 01:12:11.180
[LAUGHTER]

01:12:11.180 --> 01:12:15.230
--about the size of
a football stadium.

01:12:15.230 --> 01:12:17.960
And that would have been
scientifically interesting.

01:12:17.960 --> 01:12:20.270
[LAUGHTER]

01:12:20.270 --> 01:12:23.300
But I think Buzz
and I both agreed

01:12:23.300 --> 01:12:26.540
that on the first landing we
ought to pick something that

01:12:26.540 --> 01:12:28.790
was a little less challenging.

01:12:28.790 --> 01:12:30.740
And so we looked
for a smooth spot.

01:12:30.740 --> 01:12:33.820
And that took us a while to
get over to a smooth spot.

01:12:33.820 --> 01:12:39.680
And yeah, we did use up most
of the fuel that we had.

01:12:39.680 --> 01:12:43.010
And yes, had we had to
fly very much farther,

01:12:43.010 --> 01:12:46.020
we might have had to
abort back into orbit.

01:12:46.020 --> 01:12:49.250
But I can't say that I really
was concerned about that,

01:12:49.250 --> 01:12:51.170
because we were getting
to get down fairly

01:12:51.170 --> 01:12:52.850
close to the surface.

01:12:52.850 --> 01:12:55.490
And even if we ran
out of fuel, as long

01:12:55.490 --> 01:12:58.580
as we had the attitude
control with the 1-6G why,

01:12:58.580 --> 01:13:01.430
we thought we could
absorb that bump OK.

01:13:01.430 --> 01:13:08.090
So I really believe
that it probably

01:13:08.090 --> 01:13:09.920
wasn't a significant concern.

01:13:09.920 --> 01:13:14.360
Although Buzz was worried about
everything on the vehicle.

01:13:14.360 --> 01:13:16.260
I only had to worry
about one thing.

01:13:16.260 --> 01:13:18.730
So I should ask him
to speak for himself.

01:13:22.027 --> 01:13:23.860
AUDIENCE: Have you ever
played Lunar Lander?

01:13:23.860 --> 01:13:25.354
[LAUGHTER]

01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:35.440
ARMSTRONG: I have
to admit I have not,

01:13:35.440 --> 01:13:36.613
or at least not the same--

01:13:36.613 --> 01:13:37.875
[LAUGHTER]

01:13:37.875 --> 01:13:39.100
SEAMANS: He doesn't need to.

01:13:39.100 --> 01:13:43.000
AUDIENCE: I was curious as to
whether it was accurate at all.

01:13:43.000 --> 01:13:44.000
Thanks.

01:13:44.000 --> 01:13:46.260
ARMSTRONG: Have you, Buzz?

01:13:46.260 --> 01:13:46.840
I can't.

01:13:46.840 --> 01:13:48.527
I'm sorry.

01:13:48.527 --> 01:13:49.110
I'm a failure.

01:13:53.170 --> 01:13:56.280
AUDIENCE: Mr. Armstrong,
I was born in 1973.

01:13:56.280 --> 01:13:59.460
I was wondering if you
had any idea or thoughts

01:13:59.460 --> 01:14:01.500
to whether people would
be walking on the moon

01:14:01.500 --> 01:14:02.686
during my lifetime.

01:14:09.790 --> 01:14:15.700
ARMSTRONG: Well,
when I was your age,

01:14:15.700 --> 01:14:19.260
I was absolutely certain
that no one would walk

01:14:19.260 --> 01:14:20.790
on the moon in my lifetime.

01:14:20.790 --> 01:14:26.125
[LAUGHTER] [APPLAUSE]

01:14:35.300 --> 01:14:37.300
MURMAN: Why don't we take
just one last question

01:14:37.300 --> 01:14:38.342
from this gentleman here.

01:14:38.342 --> 01:14:39.620
And then we'll wrap it up.

01:14:39.620 --> 01:14:43.120
AUDIENCE: From the
lecture and from history

01:14:43.120 --> 01:14:44.990
it was pretty clear
that lots of systems

01:14:44.990 --> 01:14:48.020
were being tested at
once, as you described.

01:14:48.020 --> 01:14:51.230
How did that-- the people in
the program and the pilots--

01:14:51.230 --> 01:14:54.920
how did that approach to
testing, not maybe rigorously

01:14:54.920 --> 01:14:56.840
testing each system
independently,

01:14:56.840 --> 01:14:58.670
how did you react to that?

01:14:58.670 --> 01:15:01.650
What was the reaction
inside the program to that?

01:15:01.650 --> 01:15:02.400
SEAMANS: I think--

01:15:02.400 --> 01:15:04.275
ARMSTRONG: I'll talk
first, and then you can.

01:15:04.275 --> 01:15:06.310
SEAMANS: All right.

01:15:06.310 --> 01:15:09.310
ARMSTRONG: That's a
perceptive question.

01:15:09.310 --> 01:15:11.380
Those of us who
had been involved

01:15:11.380 --> 01:15:16.180
in test flying for some
period of time before this

01:15:16.180 --> 01:15:22.390
had become advocates of
the step at a time system

01:15:22.390 --> 01:15:27.460
that Dr. Seamans discussed.

01:15:27.460 --> 01:15:32.290
And it was difficult for
us to accept the idea

01:15:32.290 --> 01:15:38.380
that we were going to test
everything at the same time.

01:15:38.380 --> 01:15:40.450
Past experience with
that kind of thing--

01:15:40.450 --> 01:15:43.670
and in the aeronautical
business, some of you

01:15:43.670 --> 01:15:46.090
will know that when you
fly a new airplane that

01:15:46.090 --> 01:15:49.390
also has a new engine and also
has a new autopilot and stuff,

01:15:49.390 --> 01:15:50.950
you usually have
a lot of problems.

01:15:50.950 --> 01:15:54.610
Because you like to
have something solid.

01:15:54.610 --> 01:15:57.160
Nevertheless, what
we didn't appreciate

01:15:57.160 --> 01:16:02.020
at that point in time was the
increased confidence level

01:16:02.020 --> 01:16:06.700
that could be achieved by very
diligent component testing

01:16:06.700 --> 01:16:09.430
and systems testing
before we got to flight.

01:16:09.430 --> 01:16:11.530
And that was a level
of concentration

01:16:11.530 --> 01:16:17.340
that we had not been exposed to
and didn't previously recognize

01:16:17.340 --> 01:16:18.562
the value of.

01:16:22.330 --> 01:16:25.810
SEAMANS: Well, I can't
speak to the pilot's view

01:16:25.810 --> 01:16:30.550
of all up system testing,
but it really became obvious

01:16:30.550 --> 01:16:34.570
that we were not going
to be able to proceed

01:16:34.570 --> 01:16:38.020
to a final conclusion
of the program

01:16:38.020 --> 01:16:40.690
by just putting on a stage,
and then testing it, and then

01:16:40.690 --> 01:16:43.180
another stage, and
flying it, and so on.

01:16:43.180 --> 01:16:47.260
But I would say that we didn't
shift over in desperation.

01:16:47.260 --> 01:16:52.750
I mean, we felt that we were
taking a reasonable risk.

01:16:52.750 --> 01:16:56.470
We obviously carried out
that first Saturn V flight

01:16:56.470 --> 01:16:59.560
without anybody aboard.

01:16:59.560 --> 01:17:01.300
We weren't going to
go to that extreme.

01:17:01.300 --> 01:17:08.290
But the component testing,
it was obviously important,

01:17:08.290 --> 01:17:11.110
but the really, I
think, important testing

01:17:11.110 --> 01:17:12.970
that had not really
been done so extensively

01:17:12.970 --> 01:17:17.330
before was done down at the
Mississippi test facility,

01:17:17.330 --> 01:17:20.140
where we were able
to take a whole stage

01:17:20.140 --> 01:17:22.400
and fire up all the engines.

01:17:22.400 --> 01:17:24.940
And you have to realize
that when you did that,

01:17:24.940 --> 01:17:29.350
you had to control 7 1/2
million pounds of thrust.

01:17:29.350 --> 01:17:32.200
And that was a pretty
gigantic test facility.

01:17:32.200 --> 01:17:35.230
But we were planning
to do that anyway.

01:17:35.230 --> 01:17:39.410
And by increasing the
level of that testing,

01:17:39.410 --> 01:17:41.230
we certainly had a
lot more confidence

01:17:41.230 --> 01:17:42.860
that what we were
doing was correct.

01:17:42.860 --> 01:17:45.590
But I'll have to say that--

01:17:45.590 --> 01:17:47.620
particular that German team--

01:17:47.620 --> 01:17:49.690
were quite dubious about it.

01:17:49.690 --> 01:17:51.430
And they could not
believe it when

01:17:51.430 --> 01:17:53.950
that Saturn V, the
very first one,

01:17:53.950 --> 01:17:58.240
went all the way downrange to
where it was supposed to go

01:17:58.240 --> 01:18:01.040
and everything fired the
way it was supposed to.

01:18:01.040 --> 01:18:03.387
It was a major accomplishment.

01:18:03.387 --> 01:18:04.970
It's possibly just
a little bit lucky,

01:18:04.970 --> 01:18:07.303
but that's also important
when you're running a program.

01:18:07.303 --> 01:18:08.902
[APPLAUSE]

01:18:26.495 --> 01:18:28.870
MURMAN: I certainly sense that
the audience is joining me

01:18:28.870 --> 01:18:32.050
in thanking our speakers today
and our distinguished guests,

01:18:32.050 --> 01:18:34.688
a number of whom I have not had
a chance to introduce to you

01:18:34.688 --> 01:18:36.730
that are in the audience
that contributed heavily

01:18:36.730 --> 01:18:38.455
to the Apollo program.

01:18:38.455 --> 01:18:39.830
You've been a
wonderful audience,

01:18:39.830 --> 01:18:41.560
and thank you very
much for coming.

01:18:41.560 --> 01:18:44.010
[APPLAUSE]

